<p class="book-title">LIKUTAY HALACHOS — ORACH CHAIM, PART III</p>
<h2>Laws of Sukkah</h2>
<div class="halacha-num">Halacha 2</div>

<div class="bechina-note">
  <strong>Key terms:</strong> <em>Bechina</em> — a dimension or spiritual mode. <em>Tikun habris</em> — rectification of the covenant. <em>Tikun klali</em> — the general rectification. <em>Labnus / hitlabnus</em> — whiteness; the whitening of sins. <em>Nisuch hamayim</em> — the water libation performed in the Temple during Sukkos. <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em> — the great rejoicing of the water-drawing. <em>Hakafos</em> — the encircling processions around the altar (and on Simchas Torah around the bimah). <em>Oraisa d'atika stimaa</em> — the concealed Torah of the Ancient One; the innermost hidden dimension of Torah that will be fully revealed in the future. <em>Targum</em> — Aramaic translation; the dimension of language that mediates between holy and external. <em>Eiver haYarden</em> — the Transjordan (literally "the other side of the Jordan"); used here as a metaphor for the realm of <em>targum</em>.
</div>

<p><span class="section-label">Section 1</span>
On the first day of Sukkos one begins to take the lulav with the four species — which are the bechina of <em>tikun habris</em>. (As cited in the holy books: this is the bechina of the eighteen wavings [<em>chai ni'enu'im</em>] of the lulav — the bechina of <em>"the righteous one lives forever"</em> [<em>tzaddik chai almin</em>] — the bechina of <em>"the righteous shall flourish like a palm tree"</em> [<em>tzaddik katamar yifrach</em>].) And therefore one begins to take them on the first day of Sukkos in order to sustain the sukkah so that it should not fall, G‑d forbid — in the bechina of <em>"the fallen sukkah of David"</em> [<em>sukkas David hanofalas</em>]. For this occurs, G‑d forbid, through the bloods of the 365 sinews — which are the 365 prohibitions — being sprayed into the brain. From this comes, G‑d forbid, the falling sickness [<em>choli nofel</em>] — the bechina of the fallen sukkah, etc. And all of this is through the absence of <em>tikun habris</em>. See all of this in the teaching cited above beginning <em>"Ha'i man d'va'ay l'minsav"</em> (§29). Therefore one takes the four species of the lulav — which are the bechina of <em>tikun habris</em> — for it [the lulav] sends rectifications and whiteness to all 365 sinews. And through this we support and sustain and raise the sukkah cited above so that it should not fall, G‑d forbid, as stated.</p>

<p>And therefore they are four species — corresponding to the four white garments [<em>arba bigdei lavan</em>] which are drawn through them — through raising the <em>mochin</em> and establishing the sukkah cited above — from which whiteness is drawn, in the bechina of blood becoming turbid and turning into milk (as cited there, as stated). Which is the bechina of the sukkah — which is the bechina of <em>"call understanding 'mother'"</em> [<em>im labinah tikra</em>] — in the bechina of <em>"You sheltered me in my mother's womb"</em> [<em>tsukkeni veveten imi</em>] — for there, in the bechina of <em>"call understanding 'mother'"</em>, in the bechina of sukkah, the blood becomes turbid and turns into milk — from which the white garments are made. And corresponding to this one takes the four species of the lulav — corresponding to the four white garments made through them, as stated. And therefore one begins to take the lulav on the first day of Sukkos — for the building of the sukkah and its establishment is through the power of the whitening of the sins that was accomplished on Yom Kippur through <em>tikun habris</em>. Through this we have the power to build the sukkah and raise it, as stated.</p>

<p>And therefore immediately upon the conclusion [<em>motza'ei</em>] of Yom Kippur one begins to engage with the sukkah at once. And all four days between Yom Kippur and Sukkos one is occupied with its construction — for during all these four days sins are not reckoned against one. And therefore they are four days corresponding to the four white garments drawn on Yom Kippur — through which whiteness flows through all those four days — and therefore sins are not reckoned during them. And because of this one is occupied during all these four days with building the sukkah — for then we have the power to build the sukkah and establish it through the whitening of the sins and bloods, as stated. And therefore on the first day of Sukkos — which is the first of the reckoning of sins — we therefore fear that the sins — in the bechina of the bloods — might overwhelm and cause the bechina of the sukkah to fall, G‑d forbid, as stated. Therefore one takes the four species — through which one supports the sukkah of David so that it should not fall, G‑d forbid, as stated.</p>

<p>And for this reason one recites <em>Hallel</em> together with the lulav — for <em>Hallel</em> is the bechina of the general rectification of speech, in the bechina of <em>"bless Hashem, His angels, mighty in strength, doers of His word, to hearken,"</em> etc. — namely through praising the Righteous One of the world and glorifying Him — through this speech is rectified (as explained there in the teaching cited above). And therefore one then takes the lulav — which is also the bechina of the general rectification of the sinews. For all the bechinot of general rectifications explained there in the teaching cited above are mutually dependent. And the essential [rectification] depends on <em>tikun habris</em> — which is the totality of the sinews — which is the essential general totality of all rectifications. And this is: <em>"bless Hashem, His angels, mighty in strength, doers of His word, to hearken,"</em> etc. — which Rabbainu, may his light shine, interpreted regarding the general rectification of speech through <em>"bless Hashem"</em> — see there. And this is what is written in the verse: <em>"His angels, mighty in strength"</em> — for they are the bechina of those who guard the covenant, <em class="aramaic">"those who overpower their inclinations, who are like angels themselves"</em> [<em>inun d'misgabrin al yitzraiyhon d'damyan l'malackim mamash</em>]. And those specifically are able to rectify speech through praising the Righteous One of the world — for this depends on that, as stated. And this is the bechina of taking the lulav — which is the bechina of <em>tikun habris</em>, the totality of the sinews — during the recitation of <em>Hallel</em>, which is the praise of Hashem, blessed be He, which is the rectification of speech. For both are bechinot of the general rectification — and mutually dependent, as stated.</p>

<p>And therefore the wavings [<em>ni'enu'im</em>] — which are the essential fulfillment of the mitzvah — are performed at <em>Hodu</em> at the beginning and end [of <em>Hallel</em>]. For <em>"Give thanks to Hashem"</em> [<em>Hodu laHashem</em>] is the essential praise of the Righteous One of the world — and therefore then is the essential fulfillment of the mitzvah of lulav, which is the wavings. For the wavings are the essential <em>tikun habris</em>. And therefore there are six [directions] — to all six directions — for this is the bechina of <em>tikun habris</em>, which is the bechina of <em>vav</em>. And at each direction three wavings — making eighteen wavings in all — the bechina of <em>"the righteous one lives forever"</em> [<em>tzaddik chai almin</em>] — as is cited.</p>

<p>For the lulav is the bechina of the weapons of war to conquer and overpower the seventy nations (as it is cited in the holy Zohar) — namely through it one conquers and overpowers the encompassing evil of the seventy nations, which is the desire of licentiousness [<em>ta'avat niyuf</em>] — and one merits <em>tikun habris</em>, which is the general rectification, as stated. Which is the bechina of <em>Shaday</em> — <em class="aramaic">"Who shoots and sends rectifications like an arrow to all the narrow and constricted places"</em> [<em>shadi v'yoreh k'chetz tikunim l'chol mekomos hatzarim v'hadakim</em>] (as cited in the teaching cited above). And this is the bechina of the wavings — that one waves and shoots the weapons of war in every direction and diagonal direction, aiming the weapons of war to strike to a hair's breadth — in order to conquer and subdue them in every place they are found. And through this itself, rectifications are sent to all sides — to all the narrow and constricted places, as stated. Which is also the bechina of the wavings — that one waves and sends rectifications to all diagonal directions in the bechina of <em>Shaday</em>, as stated. For this is dependent on that — through the strengthening and victory of the war: which is the essential rectification, which is the bechina of full countervailing repentance [<em>teshuvas hamishkal mamash</em>] — through this, rectifications are sent to all the constricted places, as stated. And this is the bechina of the lulav which rises and tapers, going upward until at its tip it is very thin — to indicate that it waves and sends rectifications to all the narrow and constricted places, as stated.</p>

<p>Also in <em>Hallel</em> the subduing of the encompassing evil of the seventy nations is mentioned — in the bechina of <em>"they surrounded me"</em> [<em>sabuni</em>] etc. — <em>"in the name of Hashem I will cut them off"</em> [<em>b'Shem Hashem ki amilam</em>]. <em>Mil</em> in <em>gematria</em> = seventy nations — for this is the bechina of <em>milah</em> [circumcision] (as cited in <em>Sha'arei Orah</em>). And <em>milah</em> is the bechina of the subduing of the encompassing evil — namely the general desire cited above (as cited in the teaching <em>"Tefillah l'Chavakuk"</em> §19). Therefore one then takes the lulav, as stated — for it is the weapon of war against them, as stated.</p>

<hr class="section-div">

<p><span class="section-label">Section 2</span>
And this is the bechina of the water libation [<em>nisuch hamayim</em>] during the festival. For upon all the sacrifices they would pour wine [<em>yayin</em>] — for the sacrifices are [the bechina of] <em>tikun habris</em>, as they subdue the animal spirit which is the blemish of the covenant, as stated. And this is what is written: <em>"You shall not cut off the salt of your G‑d's covenant from your sacrifice."</em> And therefore they would pour wine then — for through <em>tikun habris</em>, wine is sweetened. And then on the contrary — through the wine, the rectification is further increased. For the brain is elevated through it in the bechina of <em>"one who merited [sobriety] becomes the head"</em> [<em>zachah na'aseh rosh</em>] (as cited in the teaching cited above). And this is why one sings over the wine — namely, songs and praises to Hashem, blessed be He — which is also the general rectification through the elevation of <em>da'as</em> that comes through praising the Righteous One of the world. And this is the bechina of the song [accompanying] the libations of the sacrifices, as stated.</p>

<p>And because during Sukkos it is then the bechina of <em>tikun habris</em> to a greater degree, as stated, and then the bloods are whitened and clarified greatly — therefore then one pours both water and wine together (as stated in the Mishnah) — to indicate that the wine has been sweetened greatly, an additional sweetening, until it has been sweetened to the bechina of water. For water is the bechina of <em>chassadim</em> [lovingkindnesses] — and they are the diluting and sweetening of the wine. And this is why the water libation is alluded to amidst the wine libations — as our Sages of blessed memory derived (Tractate Ta'anis): the word <em>"v'niskeihem"</em> [and their libations] is written in the second [day's passage with an unusual spelling] — for the water libation sweetens the wine, as stated.</p>

<hr class="section-div">

<p><span class="section-label">Section 3</span>
And therefore there is great joy in the <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em> — for when the wine is sweetened, it is then in the bechina of wine that gladdens. And from there also comes the joy of the entire festival — through meriting the sukkah, which is the bechina of <em>"call understanding 'mother'"</em>, where there is wine that gladdens. And then during <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em> one merits the attainment of wisdom greatly — as it is written: <em>"And you shall draw water with joy"</em> [<em>ushavtem mayim b'sason</em>] — and its Aramaic translation: <em class="aramaic">"and you shall receive new teaching"</em> [<em>u'sekablun ulpan chadat</em>]. For then the brain is greatly elevated in the bechina of <em>"one who merited becomes the head"</em> (as explained there). And therefore one rejoices in the drawing of the water — for they are in the bechina of its sweetening, as stated. And thereby wine that gladdens is made — which is the bechina of the attainment of wisdom, as is cited.</p>

<p>And therefore one greatly multiplies songs and praises during <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em> — for this is the bechina of rectification of speech, as stated. And therefore the elders would proclaim that they had merited to be cleansed of sins (as explained there) — what the elders would say: <em>"Fortunate is our old age that has atoned,"</em> etc. For the essential joy of <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em> — which occurs during Sukkos — is only through having merited the clarification of the bloods. Through this the wine is sweetened and mixed with water, as stated. And this is why they would say: <em>"We are for Y-H and our eyes are toward Y-H"</em> [<em>anu l'Y-H v'einainu l'Y-H</em>] — which is the bechina of the <em>mochin</em>, the bechina of <em>"acquire wisdom, acquire understanding"</em> [<em>k'neh chochmah k'neh vinah</em>] — for they are <em>Y-H</em>.</p>

<hr class="section-div">

<p><span class="section-label">Section 4</span>
And this is the bechina of the encirclings [<em>hakafos</em>] — with which they would encircle the willow [<em>aravah</em>] each day once, and on Hoshana Rabbah seven times, with the four species. For the willow is the bechina of <em>malchus-peh</em> [the mouth of kingship], the bechina of faith [<em>emunah</em>] — as is cited. And therefore they would encircle it with the lulav and four species — in order to complete the bechina of speech, the bechina of <em>malchus</em>, the bechina of faith — through the bechina of the <em>makifim</em> [encircling lights] that draw whiteness and rectification to her through them — to clothe her in the four white garments drawn from there through the four species of the lulav, as stated. And therefore on Hoshana Rabbah they would encircle seven times — corresponding to the seven layers of the cornea [<em>shivas gilday d'eina</em>] — which are in the bechina of hairs [<em>sa'aros</em>], the bechina of encircling <em>mochin</em> surrounding the pupil of the eye (as mentioned there at the end of the teaching) — which rectify the menstrual blood [<em>dam niddos</em>], as mentioned there. For all our engagement throughout the seven days of Sukkos — with sukkah and lulav and the other general rectifications cited above — all of it is in order to merit on Hoshana Rabbah to seal the outer seal [<em>chosam hachitzon</em>]: the bechina of faith [<em>emunah</em>] — which is rectified through the general rectification of speech, as stated. For faith depends upon the mouth of a person — as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote (§44 of <em>Likutay Tinyana</em>). And everything depends upon <em>tikun habris</em> — as cited in the words of Rabbainu in many places: that faith depends upon <em>tikun habris</em>. And therefore the mitzvah of the four species is performed with the hands — for they are taken in the hands because they come to rectify the seal of the hands [<em>chosam hayadayim</em>] — which is the seal of faith, as stated. And the principle: <em>tikun habris</em> and faith — which is the bechina of the mouth [<em>peh</em>] and speech — are mutually dependent. And they are the bechina of the inner seal and outer seal — which are the seal of the feet [<em>chosam haraGlayim</em>] and seal of the hands [<em>chosam hayadayim</em>] — the bechina of the seal of the covenant of the tongue [<em>chosam bris halashon</em>] and the seal of the covenant of the flesh [<em>chosam bris hama'or</em>] — which are in the bechina of <em>"He made a covenant between the ten fingers of his hands — which is the covenant of the tongue — and He made a covenant,"</em> etc. And they are in the bechina of what Rabbainu, may his light shine, said: that the guarding of the covenant and the perfection of the holy tongue are mutually dependent — for they are the bechina of the two seals cited above — a seal within a seal — each dependent on the other.</p>

<p>And therefore one stays awake on the night of Hoshana Rabbah — in order to merit the perfection of the holy tongue, which is the bechina of <em>malchus-peh</em>, etc. — whose essential completion is through the tongue of <em>targum</em> [Aramaic], which is the bechina of deep sleep [<em>tardemah</em>]. And therefore one stays awake — in order to subdue the bechina of sleep, the bechina of <em>tardemah</em> (as cited above). And this is why <em>Mishneh Torah</em> [Deuteronomy] is then recited — which is the bechina of <em>malchus</em>, as is cited — namely the bechina of <em>malchus-peh</em> cited above: the bechina of the perfection of the holy tongue through the tongue of <em>targum</em>. Which is the bechina of <em>Mishneh Torah</em> — in the bechina of <em>"These are the words that Moshe spoke on the other side of the Jordan"</em> [<em>eileh hadevarim asher diber Moshe b'ever haYarden</em>]. <em>"On the other side of the Jordan"</em> [<em>b'ever haYarden</em>] precisely — for <em>ever haYarden</em> is the bechina of the tongue of <em>targum</em> that completes the holy tongue. For the tongue of <em>targum</em> — which is the Aramaic language — is from <em>ever haYarden</em>.</p>

<p>And therefore the tribes of Israel took their portion and inheritance there as well — for the Land of Israel is the bechina of the holy tongue. For the holy tongue was chosen from all the languages of the nations, and similarly the Land of Israel was chosen from the lands of the seventy peoples. And Israel — who were chosen from the seventy nations — merited both. For the Land of Israel is also the bechina of the sanctity of the covenant — the bechina of <em>"the righteous shall inherit the land"</em> [<em>tzaddikim yirshu aretz</em>]. And it is the bechina of faith — which is the bechina of <em>malchus-peh</em> — the bechina of the perfection of the holy tongue — as it is written: <em>"Dwell in the land and cultivate faith"</em> [<em>sh'chan eretz ur'ei emunah</em>] (as Rabbainu wrote). And Israel merited their portion and inheritance — the Land of Israel — for it belongs to the people of Israel: <em>"They are all righteous; forever shall they inherit the land."</em> And since the essential completion of the holy tongue is through the tongue of <em>targum</em> — which is <em>ever haYarden</em>, the bechina of the Aramaic tongue, as stated — therefore Israel were required to also take their portion there — in order to complete the bechina of the holy tongue through the tongue of <em>targum</em> — which is the bechina of <em>ever haYarden</em>, as stated.</p>

<p>And this is why Moshe was at first greatly angered at the children of Reuven and the children of Gad for choosing <em>ever haYarden</em> — for he thought they wished to remain there permanently. Therefore he was greatly angered at them — for the bechina of the tongue of <em>targum</em> has no perfection or holiness in itself except when it completes the holy tongue. And the essential battle is through it. And therefore when they answered him that they would cross over as armed troops before their brothers — for the essential battle is through them, and therefore they specifically must cross over as armed troops before the battle — for the essential battle and victory is in the bechina of the tongue of <em>targum</em> (as cited above in the Laws of Chol HaMo'ed). And afterward, when they will have won the battle, they will return to dwell there — for then, when one fully subdues the evil of the seventy nations, the <em>targum</em> then completes the holy tongue, and then the <em>targum</em> is also holy. And therefore they can afterward dwell in <em>ever haYarden</em> — which is the bechina of <em>targum</em>, as stated. And therefore afterward Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him, was appeased regarding them.</p>

<p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: <em>"A person should always complete his Torah portions with the congregation — two Scripture and one Targum — and even Ataroth and Divon"</em> — which are cities of <em>ever haYarden</em>. And this is puzzling: why did they take specifically this verse [as their example]? After all, there is also a <em>targum</em> on this verse now. And even if we say with difficulty that in their days there was no <em>targum</em> on this verse — still, it would have been difficult for them to take the verse about Reuven and Shimon [earlier in the Torah] which has no <em>targum</em> even now, and which appears earlier in the Torah. But the innovation [<em>ribusa</em>] is: even Ataroth and Divon — which are themselves <em>ever haYarden</em>, which are themselves the bechina of <em>targum</em> — even so, one must read them with the [formula of] two Scripture and one Targum. For Torah has seventy facets, and a single verse divides into many interpretations. And therefore <em>Mishneh Torah</em> was said in <em>ever haYarden</em> — which is the bechina of the completion of the holy tongue through <em>targum</em>, as stated.</p>

<p>And therefore one reads it on the night of Hoshana Rabbah — for then one requires this, as stated. And this is why one completes all of <em>Tehillim</em> then — for the recitation of <em>Tehillim</em> is a rectification for a nocturnal emission [<em>mikrah laylah</em>] (as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote). And a nocturnal emission comes through the bechina of <em>tardemah</em> [deep sleep] — the bechina of <em>targum</em> (as cited there in the teaching <em>"Tefillah l'Chavakuk"</em>). And therefore on the night of Hoshana Rabbah — when one must rectify the bechina of the mouth [<em>peh</em>], as stated, which is the bechina of the willow [<em>aravah</em>], as stated — therefore one stays awake the entire night and breaks the sleep and reads <em>Mishneh Torah</em> and <em>Tehillim</em> — in order to merit the perfection of the holy tongue through the tongue of <em>targum</em>, as stated.</p>

<hr class="section-div">

<p><span class="section-label">Section 5</span>
And afterward — through all these rectifications through which one has merited the bechina of <em>makifim</em>, which is the completion of <em>da'as</em> — therefore afterward comes <em>Simchas Torah</em>. For then we merit the attainment of the concealed Torah [<em>Torah hastumah</em>] — the bechina of <em class="aramaic">"the Torah of the Ancient Concealed One, which is destined to be revealed in the future"</em> [<em>oraisa d'atika stimaa da'asida l'isgalya l'asid lavo</em>]. For <em>Simchas Torah</em> is the bechina of the ultimate final [level] (as cited in the holy books) — and therefore the Torah is concluded then, and one immediately begins again — to indicate that one merits the bechina of the Torah of the Ancient One, which is entirely right [<em>kula yemina</em>], with no beginning or end — only simple unity. And this is the bechina of the encircling <em>hakafos</em> performed with the Torah — in the bechina of <em>kula yemina</em>. For the one who encircles goes [circling] to the right — as stated in the Mishnah. Thus in the bechina of the <em>hakafos</em> there is no beginning or end or sides — and they are the bechina of <em>kula yemina</em>, as stated. Which is the bechina of the concealed Torah cited above — which one merits then, as stated.</p>

<p>For the essential attainment of the concealed <em>Oraisa d'Atika Stimaa</em> cited above is through the subduing of evil — as Rabbainu, of blessed memory, wrote in the teaching <em>"Mi ha'ish hechafetz chayyim"</em> (§33): that through overpowering one's inclination, one thereby subdues and nullifies the evil that rests upon the good. And through this the Torah that is hidden and concealed among the languages of the nations is revealed. And thereby one merits, as stated, the <em>Oraisa d'Atika Stimaa</em> cited above (see there carefully). And this is the bechina of the water libation [<em>nisuch hamayim</em>] alluded to amidst the libations of the bulls — which correspond to the seventy nations, which diminish progressively [during the seven days of Sukkos] as our Sages of blessed memory said — to indicate that the hidden Torah is clothed among those nations. And therefore when one subdues them — so that they diminish progressively — the Torah hidden among them that was concealed until now is revealed. For the water is in the bechina of the concealed Torah cited above — as it is written: <em>"And you shall draw water with joy"</em> [<em>ushavtem mayim b'sason</em>] — and its Aramaic translation: <em class="aramaic">"and you shall receive new teaching"</em> [<em>u'sekablun ulpan chadat</em>]. And therefore this [water libation] is done during Sukkos — when it is the subduing of the evil of all seventy nations through the rectifications performed then, as stated. And this is the bechina of <em>Simchas Beis Hasho'aivah</em>. And this is the bechina of <em>Simchas Torah</em> — that one rejoices greatly, having merited to attain the <em>Oraisa Stimaa</em> through the victory of the battle and the rectifications of the days of Sukkos, as stated.</p>

<p class="colophon">Blessed be Hashem forever, Amen and Amen.</p>

<div class="translator-note">
  <h4>Translator's Note</h4>
  <p>This halacha traces a single unbroken arc from Yom Kippur through Simchas Torah — each element of the Sukkos season revealed as a link in one continuous chain of rectification.</p>
  <p>The chain: Yom Kippur produces the inner seal (<em>chosam pnimi</em>) through <em>tikun habris</em>. The whitening of sins on Yom Kippur empowers the four days of sukkah-building. On the first day of Sukkos — when the sins-reckoning resumes — the four species are taken to sustain the sukkah (<em>sukkas David hanofalas</em>) through their 18 wavings, sending rectifications to all 365 sinews like <em>Shaday</em> shooting arrows into narrow places. The <em>Hallel</em> accompanies the lulav because both are forms of the same <em>tikun klali</em>: one rectifies the sinews, the other rectifies speech. On Hoshana Rabbah the seven circuits around the willow complete the outer seal (<em>chosam chitzon</em>) — <em>emunah</em>. The night vigil with <em>Mishneh Torah</em> and <em>Tehillim</em> subdues the <em>tardemah</em>/<em>targum</em> dimension, completing holy speech. And the ultimate fruit: <em>Simchas Torah</em> — the revealed joy of the <em>Oraisa d'Atika Stimaa</em>, the hidden Torah that can only emerge when the evil of the seventy nations has been subdued. This is why the Sukkos bull-offerings diminish daily (70 total): they represent the nations being progressively subdued until the hidden Torah within them is freed.</p>
  <p>The water libation (<em>nisuch hamayim</em>) unifies all of this: wine = <em>gevurah</em>/din; water = <em>chassadim</em>/sweetness. During Sukkos, the <em>tikun habris</em> is so complete that the wine has been sweetened to the point of water — hence both are poured together. <em>"You shall draw water with joy"</em> = <em>"you shall receive new teaching"</em> (Targum) = the hidden Torah of the future, revealed now through the joy of those who have fought the battle and won.</p>
